From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bootutil_misc: reject an erased swap_size trailer field
 instead of returning 0 (#1066)

boot_read_swap_size() is a raw, unchecked flash_area_read() -- it hands
callers whatever bytes are at the trailer's swap_size offset, with no
erased-value check.  Both of its callers (loader.c's boot_swap_image()
resume branch, and swap_scratch.c's boot_read_image_header()) only reach
it once a GOOD magic has already established that a swap trailer is
present, so in every reachable case the field should hold a real,
nonzero swap_size.

On a flash device whose erase value is 0x00 (Alif Ensemble on-die MRAM,
FLASH_MRAM_ERASE_VALUE in drivers/flash/flash_mram_alif.c), that erase
value is bit-for-bit identical to a genuine swap_size of 0 -- unlike the
obviously-invalid 0xffffffff a 0xFF-erase device would read back for the
same "not really written" field.  A stale or partially/torn-written
swap_size then reads back as 0 instead of as clearly-erased, and
copy_size == 0 degenerates swap_scratch.c's algorithm silently:

  * find_last_sector_idx(state, 0) returns -1 immediately (its break
    condition is trivially satisfied at 0 >= 0 && 0 >= 0 && 0 == 0), so
    swap_run()'s loop -- the only call site of boot_swap_sectors(), where
    the scratch-trailer erase lives -- never runs.  A swapped board's
    scratch magic is then never cleared, and boot_status_source() keeps
    selecting BOOT_STATUS_SOURCE_SCRATCH on every following boot: the
    device resumes the same no-op swap forever.

  * find_swap_count(state, 0) (called from boot_read_image_header(),
    earlier in the boot flow) also returns 0 for the same reason, which
    unconditionally satisfies boot_read_image_header()'s
    "bs->idx - BOOT_STATUS_IDX_0 >= swap_count" branch and substitutes
    the WRONG slot's header for the one requested -- reproduced on the
    mcuboot host simulator (see the companion sim/ regression patch):
    the unpatched read segfaults when a genuinely in-progress swap's
    swap_size field collides with the erase value this way.

Make the read itself refuse the ambiguity: after a successful
flash_area_read(), treat a swap_size field that reads as this device's
erase value (bootutil_buffer_is_erased(), the same helper
boot_read_flag() already uses for the image_ok/copy_done flags) as a
read failure (BOOT_EBADSTATUS) rather than handing out a meaningless 0.
Both existing call sites already fail safely on a nonzero return here
without any further change: loader.c's boot_swap_image() resume branch
has `assert(rc == 0)` immediately after the call, which -- one level up,
in boot_prepare_status() -- makes boot_read_image_headers() fail and the
swap is declined outright (BOOT_SWAP_TYPE_NONE, primary left untouched,
BOOT_LOG_WRN'd) instead of proceeding on a 0; swap_scratch.c's
boot_read_image_header() already turns any nonzero return here into
BOOT_EFLASH before ever computing find_swap_count()'s wrong-slot
substitution.  This is a single-choke-point fix rather than a per-call-
site guard precisely so both consumers of the same ambiguous read are
covered by one change, not just the one #1066 was filed against.

Signed-off-by: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
---
diff --git a/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_misc.c b/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_misc.c
index b64ceab..dce9003 100644
--- a/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_misc.c
+++ b/boot/bootutil/src/bootutil_misc.c
@@ -211,8 +211,36 @@ boot_read_swap_size(const struct flash_area *fap, uint32_t *swap_size)
 
     off = boot_swap_size_off(fap);
     rc = flash_area_read(fap, off, swap_size, sizeof *swap_size);
+    if (rc != 0) {
+        return rc;
+    }
 
-    return rc;
+    /*
+     * Both callers (loader.c boot_swap_image() resume branch and
+     * swap_scratch.c boot_read_image_header()) only reach this function
+     * once a GOOD magic has already established that a swap trailer is
+     * present -- a genuine swap in progress always carries a real,
+     * nonzero swap_size. On a flash device whose erase value is 0x00
+     * (e.g. Alif Ensemble on-die MRAM,
+     * drivers/flash/flash_mram_alif.c FLASH_MRAM_ERASE_VALUE), an
+     * erased -- or partially/never-written -- swap_size field is
+     * bit-for-bit identical to a legitimate swap_size of 0, unlike the
+     * obviously-invalid 0xffffffff a 0xFF-erase device would read back.
+     * Treat that ambiguity as a read failure rather than handing
+     * callers a 0: find_last_sector_idx()/find_swap_count() both
+     * degenerate a copy_size/swap_size of 0 into a silent no-op
+     * (find_last_sector_idx returns -1 immediately), so a genuinely
+     * in-progress swap could otherwise be reported as nothing to do
+     * instead of failing loudly. Both call sites already fail safely on
+     * a nonzero return here -- loader.c asserts, swap_scratch.c
+     * propagates BOOT_EFLASH -- so this adds no new error-handling
+     * surface, it only refuses to hand out a meaningless zero (#1066).
+     */
+    if (bootutil_buffer_is_erased(fap, swap_size, sizeof *swap_size)) {
+        return BOOT_EBADSTATUS;
+    }
+
+    return 0;
 }
 
 #ifdef MCUBOOT_SWAP_USING_OFFSET
